    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/theseus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theseus.jpg" alt="Theseus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/theseus">Theseus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kylix_Theseus_Aison_MNA_Inv11365_n1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aison, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> was inhabited continuously from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> the Acropolis carried a fortified Mycenaean palace. Unlike many Mycenaean centres it was not destroyed around 1200 BCE, and in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> it remained one of the largest communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, known for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protogeometric-style">Protogeometric</a> and Geometric pottery from the cemetery of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerameikos">Kerameikos</a>. Over the following centuries the villages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>, an area of some 2,500 square kilometres, were drawn into a single state centred on Athens, a process the Athenians credited to the hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theseus">Theseus</a> and called the <em>synoikismos</em>. Kingship gave way to rule by aristocratic magistrates; the list of annual archons begins in 683/682 BCE. An attempt by the nobleman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cylon-of-athens">Cylon</a> to seize power as tyrant, probably in 632 BCE, failed, and in 621 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/draco-lawgiver">Draco</a> gave the city its first written laws, remembered for their severity. Debt and land shortage then brought Attica close to civil war, and in 594 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solon">Solon</a> was appointed archon to mediate. His reforms cancelled debts secured on persons, abolished debt bondage and divided citizens into four property classes, the basis of the constitution that later reformers built upon.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/piraeus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/piraeus.jpg" alt="Piraeus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/piraeus">Piraeus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2011_Dimos_Pireos.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pitichinaccio, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> probably held 250,000–300,000 people, of whom perhaps 30,000–60,000 were adult male citizens; the rest were citizens' families, resident foreigners known as metics, and a large enslaved population that worked in households, workshops and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a>' law of 451 BCE restricted citizenship to children of two Athenian parents. Most citizens farmed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> was the chief agricultural export, while Attic black-figure and then red-figure pottery dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> markets in the sixth and fifth centuries. The rich silver mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laurion">Laurion</a> supplied the tetradrachms stamped with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>'s owl, among the most trusted coins of the Greek world, and a new vein found in 483 BCE paid for the fleet. Attica could not feed itself, so the harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piraeus">Piraeus</a>, laid out as a planned town in the fifth century, imported grain from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, and securing that supply shaped Athenian policy for centuries. Religious life centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena-polias">Athena Polias</a>, honoured every four years in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panathenaea">Great Panathenaia</a>, and on festivals such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysia">City Dionysia</a>, where tragedies and comedies were staged, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusinian-mysteries">Eleusinian Mysteries of Demeter</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Kore</a>. Women were excluded from political life but held important priesthoods and roles in cult.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marathon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-marathon.jpg" alt="Battle of Marathon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marathon">Battle of Marathon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scene_of_the_Battle_of_Marathon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Steeple Davis, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the sixth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> fought as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplite</a> state, its heavy infantry drawn from citizens able to afford armour. In 490 BCE, under the generalship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miltiades">Miltiades</a>, about 10,000 Athenians and Plataeans defeated the Persian landing force at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marathon">Battle of Marathon</a>. At the urging of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a> the city then built a fleet of some 200 triremes, which formed the core of the Greek navy that defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-salamis">Battle of Salamis</a> in 480 BCE, and Athenians fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> in 479 BCE. Naval power, rowed largely by the poorest citizens, the thetes, became the basis of Athenian strength and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a>: under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon</a> the allies destroyed a Persian fleet and army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-eurymedon">Battle of the Eurymedon</a> around 466 BCE. The Long Walls joining Athens to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piraeus">Piraeus</a>, completed around 457 BCE, made the city a fortified island. A great expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> ended in disaster around 454 BCE. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> of 431–404 BCE Athens survived a devastating plague and won at Pylos in 425 BCE, but lost most of a vast armament in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-expedition">Sicilian Expedition</a> of 415–413 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysander">Lysander</a>'s destruction of its last fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-aegospotami">Aegospotami</a> in 405 BCE forced surrender.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pericles"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pericles.jpg" alt="Pericles" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pericles_Pio-Clementino_Inv269_n2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Copy of Kresilas, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes">Cleisthenes</a>' reforms of 508/507 BCE, after the fall of the tyrant Hippias, created the democracy. Citizens were registered in about 139 local demes, grouped into ten new tribes that cut across regional loyalties, and each tribe supplied fifty members, chosen by lot, to the Council of 500, which prepared business for the Assembly, or <em>ekklesia</em>, of all adult male citizens. Most magistracies were filled by lot for one year, while the ten generals, the <em>strategoi</em>, were elected and could be re-elected, which allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> to lead the city as general for much of the period from 443 to 429 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostracism">Ostracism</a>, first used in 487 BCE, let the Assembly exile a leading politician for ten years. In 462/461 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephialtes-athenian-statesman">Ephialtes</a> stripped the aristocratic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/areopagus">Council of the Areopagus</a> of most of its powers, and large popular juries, paid from public funds, took over most judicial business. The democracy was overthrown twice, by the oligarchy of the Four Hundred in 411 BCE and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">Thirty Tyrants</a> installed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> in 404 BCE, but was restored in 403 BCE and lasted, with pay for attending the Assembly and a code of laws revised by legislative boards, until the Macedonian regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> imposed a property qualification in 322 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/parthenon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parthenon.jpg" alt="Parthenon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Parthenon_in_Athens.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Steve Swayne, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> is the best documented Greek city and the source of much of what later ages regarded as Classical. Its democracy, the most radical of the ancient world, became a reference point, admired or feared, for political thinkers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> to the eighteenth-century revolutionaries. The building programme begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a> (447–432 BCE) designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ictinus">Ictinus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callicrates-of-leontion">Callicrates</a> with sculpture overseen by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phidias">Phidias</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/propylaea">Propylaea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erechtheion">Erechtheion</a>, set a standard for architecture copied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and again from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> onward. Athenian festivals produced tragedy and comedy: the surviving plays of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophocles">Sophocles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes">Aristophanes</a> remain the foundation of Western drama. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> is said to have given readings of his work in Athens, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> wrote the history of its war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a>, tried and executed in 399 BCE, left his mark through his pupils; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/platonic-academy">Academy</a> around 387 BCE, Aristotle the Lyceum in 335 BCE, and Zeno and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epicurus">Epicurus</a> opened their Stoic and Epicurean schools around 300 BCE, making Athens the philosophical capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> into Roman times. The Attic dialect became the basis of the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">koine</a></em>, the common Greek of the Hellenistic world and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaeronea-338-bce"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaeronea-338-bce.jpg" alt="Battle of Chaeronea (338 BCE)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaeronea-338-bce">Battle of Chaeronea (338 BCE)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Chaeronea,_338_BC_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kirill Lokshin; derivative work by Dipa_1965, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Defeat in 404 BCE ended the Athenian empire, but the city recovered quickly. It founded the Second Athenian League in 378 BCE, though the revolt of Chios, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, Cos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-357-355-bce">Social War</a> of 357–355 BCE weakened it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, urged on by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes-general">Demosthenes</a>, joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> and was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaeronea-338-bce">Battle of Chaeronea</a> in 338 BCE; Philip treated the city leniently, and it joined his League of Corinth. After Alexander's death its revolt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamian-war">Lamian War</a> failed, and in 322 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> placed a garrison in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piraeus">Piraeus</a> and restricted citizenship. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-of-phalerum">Demetrius of Phalerum</a> governed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> from 317 to 307 BCE, and after defeat in the Chremonidean War <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-ii-gonatas">Antigonus II Gonatas</a> occupied the city in 262 BCE. Athens bought out the Macedonian garrison in 229 BCE and drew close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which gave it the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delos">Delos</a> as a free port in 167/166 BCE. In 88 BCE, under the leadership of Athenion and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristion">Aristion</a>, Athens sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a> against Rome. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> besieged the city and stormed it on 1 March 86 BCE, ending its independent political life, although Athens survived as a privileged free city and university town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> matters as the birthplace of democracy and the leading city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classical-greece">Classical Greece</a>: its navy defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and built an Aegean empire, while its drama, philosophy, historiography and architecture shaped Hellenistic, Roman and later European culture long after the city lost its political independence.</em></p>

